    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "EVELA\nFIELD\nMAY\nROOS\n20\n1977\n11530\n1 N\nCharles Augustus Lindbergh\nWith youthful courage and the Spirit of St. Louis\nhis was the first solo flight of the Atlantic\n1927 * May 20-21 * 1977\nUSA • 13c\nFIRST DAY OF ISSUE\nUSA - 13C\n50th Anniversary Solo Transatlantic Flight",
      "OFFICIAL COMMEMORATIVE\nINTERNATIONAL AEROSPACE HALL OF FAME\nHeelwood\nCHARLES AUGUSTUS LINDBERGH\n(1902 - 1974)\nWhen Charles Lindbergh was born, no one could have\nField, Long Island, into an uncertain fate over the\npredicted that twenty-five years later he would become\nAtlantic. Alone, without radio or sextant, and guided\none of the greatest American heroes of all time. His\nonly by a compass and a map of the North Atlantic, he\naccomplishment, the first solo non-stop flight from New\ncaptured the hearts and won the prayers of the world.\nYork to Paris, was the dream of aviators from the\nThirty-three hours after barely clearing telephone wires at\nmoment man first flew. Encouraged by the $25,000 Orteig\nRoosevelt Field, he trundled to a stop at Le Bourget\nPrize, it was a challenge which by May 20, 1927, had cost\nAerodrome in Paris cheered by a hundred thousand\nfive lives and had attracted organizations with huge\nParisians. For people of all countries, Lindbergh's flight\naeroplanes and platoons of aviators. Then, a tall,\nwas truly a victory of proportion. A deed which\nhandsome, all-American boy — actually a stunt flyer\nreaffirmed mankind's faith in itself. An event which is\nturned airmail pilot — arrived out of nowhere with his\ncelebrated by this First Day Cover issued on the precise\nhomely plane, the Spirit of St. Louis. On the morning of\nFiftieth Anniversary of the beginning of this historic\nMay 20, 1927, he bounced down a muddy Roosevelt\nflight at the place where the story began.\n2012.149.1541"
